{
  "tema": 6261,
  "slug": "no-he-comido-no-voy-a",
  "titulo": "Negation with no in compound verb forms",
  "subtitulo": "«No lo he visto.» «No voy a ir.»",
  "foto": "t02.jpg",
  "regla": "No goes in front of the whole verb group: before the conjugated verb (no he comido, no voy a ir, no puedo salir, no estoy trabajando) and before any pronoun in front of it (no lo he visto). For a plain negative it never goes in the middle.",
  "comparada_kwiziq": null,
  "secciones": [
    {
      "id": "regla",
      "titulo": "The rule",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "In Spanish, **no** goes right before the **conjugated verb**. In a verb group (two or more verbs together), that means **no** goes at the very front, not in the middle like English _not_."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "tabla",
          "encabezados": [
            "Structure",
            "Affirmative",
            "Negative"
          ],
          "filas": [
            [
              "haber + participle",
              "He comido.",
              "**No** he comido."
            ],
            [
              "ir a + infinitive",
              "Voy a salir.",
              "**No** voy a salir."
            ],
            [
              "estar + gerund",
              "Estoy trabajando.",
              "**No** estoy trabajando."
            ],
            [
              "poder, querer, deber + infinitive",
              "Puedo ir.",
              "**No** puedo ir."
            ],
            [
              "tener que + infinitive",
              "Tengo que pagar.",
              "**No** tengo que pagar."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "If an object or reflexive pronoun comes before the verb, **no** goes before the pronoun: **no lo** he visto, **no me** voy a quedar."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "formula",
          "partes": [
            "no",
            "+ (pronoun) +",
            "verb group"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "If the pronoun is stuck to the end of the infinitive or gerund, **no** still opens the group: **no** voy a hacer**lo**, **no** estoy viéndo**la**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "**Haber** and the participle never split: nothing goes between **he** and **comido**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "**No** after **ir a**, **poder** or **querer** (**voy a no ir**, **puedo no ir**) is possible, but it means something different: _I'm going to (choose to) not go_, _I'm allowed not to go_. For a plain negative, **no** goes first."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Tip: **no** is the bouncer. It stands at the door, in front of the whole group."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
